Opengrep taint A/B - ARM OFF (the control: taint analysis not enabled)
harness/artifacts/logs/taint-ab-off.log
================================================================================

================================================================================
WHAT THIS FILE ESTABLISHES
================================================================================

  The CONTROL arm of the controlled A/B that asks whether Opengrep's taint
  engine is active on Spark's own Scala. It carries this arm's exact command,
  the one setting by which it differs from the ON arm, the engine and ruleset
  identity it ran under, its exit code, its wall time, the findings it produced
  or did not, and its console output verbatim.

  This arm is the independence. An A/B proves a capability only through the
  CONTRAST between two invocations differing in one setting, so the ON arm's
  finding at line 72 means one thing if this arm is silent and something else
  entirely if it is not. Without a control, that finding could have come from
  plain pattern matching. AAP 0.1.3 is explicit that enabled-in-config is not
  proof - only the contrast is - and this file is the half of the contrast that
  cannot be inferred from the other half.

  So it is not a formality and its result is not assumed. THE EXPECTED RESULT
  WAS ZERO FINDINGS. STATUS, immediately below, reports what was actually
  measured, and PHASE 5 reports the consequence.

================================================================================
WHY THIS FILE IS UNDER logs/ AND NEVER UNDER raw/
================================================================================

  Two independent reasons, either of which is sufficient (AAP 0.5.1, 0.6.1):

  1. raw/ is RUNNER-ONLY. It receives exactly one artifact per tool that writes
     one and nothing else, ever. This arm's SARIF is therefore
     harness/artifacts/logs/taint-ab-off.sarif: an A/B artifact landing in raw/
     could overwrite the real opengrep.sarif the Stage 3 runner writes to
     /tmp/blitzy/blitzy-spark/blitzy-f38258d3-f87d-44f5-bedc-af512c69e0ab-w-034_c75732/harness/artifacts/raw/opengrep.sarif,
     and the dataset would then be built from an A/B over one file rather than
     from a scan over the whole scope.

  2. This is one half of one of the run's TWO DELIBERATE SECOND APPEARANCES.
     Opengrep appears here as the taint A/B subject and again as one of the
     nine scanned runners; Joern appears as a runner and again as the
     capability probe's subject. In each case the second appearance writes
     outside raw/ and contributes NO dataset row. Folding either arm into
     findings.json would corrupt both Opengrep's count and the dataset total
     (AAP 0.3.2), and reading the double appearance as a duplication would
     corrupt both counts (AAP 0.1.1).

  PHASE 6 turns both reasons into checks rather than leaving them as intentions.

