================================================================================
HOW TO READ THIS FILE
================================================================================

  Plain text, append-only, in execution order. A command line is introduced by
  '$ ', followed by what it produced:

      [stdout]  verbatim; nothing follows the marker when the stream was empty
      [stderr]  verbatim
      [exit]    the exit status, then the UTC start time and the seconds taken

  Every figure is spliced from a capture file, so no measurement is transcribed
  by hand into prose. This arm's complete console streams are reproduced without
  indentation in APPENDIX A and APPENDIX B, and the ON arm re-run's in APPENDIX C
  and APPENDIX D, so any count in PHASE 3 or PHASE 4 can be re-derived from the
  tool's own bytes. The argv vectors and their diffs are in APPENDIX E; the
  engine's own documentation of its taint options is in APPENDIX F.

  Each command ran with harness/env.sh sourced in a fresh non-login shell from
  the repository root, and with its working directory set to /opt/spark-src -
  the same convention the provisioned runner uses, so a SARIF uri is
  root-relative rather than absolute.

  No time budget applies to anything here (AAP 0.8.1). The elapsed seconds are
  facts, not measurements against a target, and nothing below compares them to
  one.

  A figure attributed to the ON arm is marked as quoted from
  harness/artifacts/logs/taint-ab-on.log. A figure attributed to the ON RE-RUN
  was measured here, in this clone, by this run. The two are never mixed.

================================================================================
METHOD - THE FOUR PRINCIPLES THIS FILE IS HELD TO
================================================================================

  review_rules returned exactly 'No user rules provided.' for the whole
  document, read in one pass with view_range=[1,-1]. That is a COMPLETE read,
  corroborated by AAP 0.7 and 0.10.2: no rules document exists for this project.
  No user-specified rule governs this file, and the absence is not read as
  licence to lower the bar - enterprise best practice is substituted, held to
  the AAP's four principles.

  P1 Verification is independent of the thing verified (AAP 0.1.3). This file IS
  that independence: it exists so the ON arm's finding is judged against a
  measured control rather than against an expectation. Two further steps keep
  the independence real rather than nominal. The engine's --help was read to
  establish which discriminator exists (PHASE 1) instead of assuming a flag
  name. And the ON arm was RE-RUN here, in this clone, in the same shell
  seconds after the control - so the contrast in PHASE 4 does not rest on a
  figure quoted from another clone's file.

  P2 Reject rather than infer (AAP 0.1.3). The control's finding is recorded as
  observed, with its line, its trace and its byte-identical artifact. The
  temptation this principle exists to refuse is to narrow the rule, move to a
  file where the arms do differ, or drop the trace flag until the count reaches
  zero. This run did none of those: the mandated subject file, the mandated
  rule and the ON arm's own flag set are what ran.

  P3 Halt-and-report over silent repair (AAP 0.8.1, 0.9.2). The failed contrast
  is stated as a halt-class finding in STATUS, at the top of the file rather
  than in a footnote, and nothing was retried to make it go away.

  P4 Verbatim preservation. Opengrep's console output is retained exactly as
  emitted, including its box-drawing characters, its trailing padding and the
  warning it prints about the internal flag the provisioned configuration bakes.

  And the standing prohibition: never fabricate a measurement. A control arm
  asserted rather than run is not a control - it is a sentence claiming a
  capability nobody verified. Every number in this file has a capture file
  behind it and a command that produced it.

================================================================================
PHASE 1 - HOW 'TAINT OFF' WAS CONSTRUCTED
================================================================================

  The A/B needs one setting to vary, and 'off' has to be the strongest off the
  engine admits. Which setting that is had to be READ from the provisioned
  configuration and from the engine, not assumed (AAP 0.4.1: invocation flags
  are read, not assumed).

  FIRST, THE PROVISIONED RUNNER. harness/bin/run-opengrep.sh bakes NO taint
  flag, so it can supply neither arm's discriminator. Its invocation, at
  harness/bin/run-opengrep.sh lines 64-70:

  [ harness/bin/run-opengrep.sh, the provisioned invocation ]
  --- begin verbatim -------------------------------------------------------------
opengrep scan "${CFG[@]}" \
  --sarif --sarif-output "$ART" \
  --timeout 120 --timeout-threshold 0 \
  --max-target-bytes 20000000 \
  --x-ignore-semgrepignore-files \
  --disable-version-check \
  "${DIRS[@]}" > "$OUT" 2> "$ERR"
  --- end verbatim ---------------------------------------------------------------

  Nothing in it was edited to add a discriminator - AAP 0.8.1 forbids editing a
  runner or its baked flags, and the sanctioned levers are environment values
  only. Every engine-configuration flag it does bake is carried into BOTH arms
  verbatim (--timeout 120, --timeout-threshold 0, --max-target-bytes 20000000,
  --x-ignore-semgrepignore-files, --disable-version-check), so the A/B runs
  under the same engine configuration as the Stage 3 scan rather than a
  configuration invented for the experiment.

  SECOND, THE ENGINE. Every taint-related option Opengrep 1.27.1 offers, from
  its own `opengrep scan --help` (558 lines, 22619 bytes; captured verbatim in
  APPENDIX F):

  [ $ grep -n -E '^ +--(no-)?[a-z-]*taint[a-z-]*$' <opengrep scan --help> ]
  --- begin verbatim -------------------------------------------------------------
192:       --guarded-taint-signatures
445:       --taint-intrafile
  --- end verbatim ---------------------------------------------------------------

  That is the complete set, and it settles three things:

    - --taint-intrafile is the only usable discriminator, and Scala is in its
      own supported-language list (APPENDIX F).
    - --guarded-taint-signatures REQUIRES --experimental, which this run does
      not use.
    - THERE IS NO FLAG THAT DISABLES TAINT. No --no-taint exists;
      --optimizations=none turns off optimizations rather than taint; and the
      --pro / --pro-path-sensitive family requires the proprietary Semgrep Pro
      Engine, deliberately unlicensed and unused here (AAP 0.3.2). All three
      are quoted from the engine's own help in APPENDIX F.

  CONSEQUENCE, STATED PLAINLY RATHER THAN PAPERED OVER: this OFF arm is
  INTRAPROCEDURAL TAINT, not taint-free. Omitting --taint-intrafile is the
  strongest 'off' this engine admits at this pin. PHASE 5 carries that as
  divergence D2, and a reader must not read this arm as a
  pattern-matching-only control.

  THIRD, THE TRACE. --dataflow-traces is passed on BOTH arms - it is not the
  variable. Its own text: 'Explain how non-local values reach the location of a
  finding (only affects text and SARIF output).' It changes what is REPORTED
  about a finding, never whether the finding is found, which is exactly why it
  belongs on both arms: it is what makes a trace visible when one exists.
  Dropping it from this arm would have changed two things at once and destroyed
  the experiment.

  FOURTH, THE OUTPUT CHANNEL. Both arms pass --sarif-output only, not the
  runner's --sarif: 'Write a copy of the SARIF output to a file or post to URL.'
  The SARIF is therefore written to a file while the console keeps the engine's
  human-readable text format - which is what makes APPENDIX A a readable console
  record with the trace spelled out instead of one very long JSON line.

  FIFTH, THE RULE. One rule, the same pinned rule the ON arm used, a taint-mode
  rule that matches the construct at the mandated line:

  [ $ grep -nE '^rules:|^- id:|^  severity:|^  mode:|^  languages:' <the rule file> ]
  --- begin verbatim -------------------------------------------------------------
1:rules:
2:- id: tainted-sql-string
3:  languages:
5:  severity: ERROR
6:  mode: taint
  --- end verbatim ---------------------------------------------------------------

  A `mode: taint` rule has no top-level pattern to match. It is evaluated as
  pattern-sources -> pattern-sinks with pattern-sanitizers, so it cannot fire at
  all unless the taint engine evaluates reachability. That is a property of the
  rule rather than a substitute for the A/B, and PHASE 5 says so: it explains
  why BOTH arms fire, and it is precisely why the pair fails to discriminate.

