  of ordering. It is ZERO: the flag changed nothing about the result on this
  file, down to the byte, on three separate captures.

  THE INTERPRETATION, STATED PLAINLY - THE SENTENCE THIS FILE EXISTS TO SUPPORT:

      Zero findings here, alongside one traced finding at line 72 in the ON arm,
      is what would establish that the taint engine - rather than pattern
      matching - produced the ON arm's result. That is the only thing that could
      establish it, because a flag set in a configuration is not proof
      (AAP 0.1.3).

      This run did not observe zero here. It observed 1 finding at line 72, traced,
      from a byte-identical artifact. THE PROOF THEREFORE DOES NOT HOLD ON THIS
      SUBJECT FILE: the ON arm's finding is not evidence of taint reachability
      that pattern-independent analysis alone could not have produced, because
      the control produced exactly the same thing. What the pair establishes is
      that the mandated subject cannot discriminate the two settings, and that
      is a halt-class finding rather than a proof (AAP 0.9.2).

  What the ON arm's finding DOES still establish on its own, stated so no reader
  has to infer it: the taint engine evaluated this file at all. The finding came
  from a `mode: taint` rule, which has no top-level pattern and is evaluated only
  as source-to-sink reachability, and the result carries a codeFlows trace whose
  steps name the source and the sink - a pattern match emits no codeFlow. What
  it does NOT establish is the CONTRAST, and the contrast is what AAP 0.1.3
  requires. This file claims what it measured and nothing beyond it.

